                            id: 'html-fund-html-tags',
                            name: 'HTML Tags',
                            beginner: [
                                {
                                    content: `<h3>What is a Tag?</h3>
• A tag is the <b>marker</b> written between angle brackets.<br>
• &lt;p&gt; is an opening tag. &lt;/p&gt; is a closing tag.<br>
• Tags are instructions to the browser, and are never shown to the user.<br><br>

<h3>Rules for Tags</h3>
• Always write tags in <b>lowercase</b> - it is the universal convention.<br>
• Most tags come in pairs.<br>
• Void tags such as &lt;br&gt; and &lt;img&gt; have no pair.<br>
• Close tags in the <b>reverse order</b> you opened them.<br><br>

<h3>Common Tags to Know First</h3>
• h1 to h6 - headings.<br>
• p - paragraph.<br>
• a - link.<br>
• img - image.<br>
• ul, ol, li - lists.<br>
• div - generic block container.<br>
• span - generic inline container.`,
                                    codeExamples: [
                                        `<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head><meta charset="UTF-8"><title>Common Tags</title></head>
<body>

  <h1>Main Heading</h1>
  <h2>Sub Heading</h2>

  <p>A paragraph of text.</p>

  <a href="https://javaschoollab.com">A link</a>

  <img src="logo.png" alt="Logo">

  <ul>
    <li>Unordered item</li>
  </ul>

  <ol>
    <li>Numbered item</li>
  </ol>

  <div>A block level container</div>
  <span>An inline container</span>

</body>
</html>`,
                                    ]
                                }
                            ],
                            intermediate: [
                                {
                                    content: `<h3>Tags You Do Not Have to Close</h3>
• HTML5 allows some closing tags to be <b>omitted</b> because the browser infers them.<br>
• &lt;/p&gt; is implied by the next block element.<br>
• &lt;/li&gt; is implied by the next &lt;li&gt;.<br>
• &lt;/td&gt; and &lt;/tr&gt; are implied by the next cell or row.<br><br>

<h3>But Always Close Them Anyway</h3>
• Omitted tags make code harder to read and to review.<br>
• Linters and formatters expect them.<br>
• A missing tag in the wrong place silently changes your DOM tree.<br>
• The cost of typing four characters is far lower than the cost of one confusing bug.<br><br>

<h3>Self-Closing Syntax</h3>
• &lt;br /&gt; with a slash is XHTML style.<br>
• In HTML5 the slash is <b>allowed but ignored</b> on void elements.<br>
• On a normal element such as &lt;div /&gt; it is <b>meaningless</b> - the div stays open.`,
                                    codeExamples: [
                                        `<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head><meta charset="UTF-8"><title>Closing Tags</title></head>
<body>

  <!-- Legal but hard to read: closing tags omitted -->
  <ul>
    <li>One
    <li>Two
    <li>Three
  </ul>

  <!-- Preferred: always close explicitly -->
  <ul>
    <li>One</li>
    <li>Two</li>
    <li>Three</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- The slash on a void element is ignored, both are identical -->
  <br>
  <br />

  <!-- DANGER: the slash does NOT close a normal element.
       This div stays OPEN and swallows the rest of the page. -->
  <!-- <div class="card" /> -->

  <!-- Correct -->
  <div class="card"></div>

</body>
</html>`,
                                    ]
                                }
                            ],
                            expert: [
                                {
                                    content: `<h3>Custom Elements</h3>
• You may invent your own tags, but the name <b>must contain a hyphen</b>.<br>
• The hyphen guarantees your tag can never clash with a future standard tag.<br>
• &lt;user-card&gt; is valid. &lt;usercard&gt; is not a valid custom element name.<br><br>

<h3>Unknown Tags</h3>
• A browser does not reject a tag it has never seen.<br>
• It creates an <b>HTMLUnknownElement</b> and styles it as inline by default.<br>
• That is why an unstyled custom tag appears to do nothing.<br>
• Give it display: block in CSS and it behaves normally.<br><br>

<h3>Why This Matters</h3>
• This forgiving behaviour is how new HTML tags can be introduced safely.<br>
• Old browsers simply treat &lt;main&gt; as an unknown inline element rather than crashing.`,
                                    codeExamples: [
                                        `<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Custom Elements</title>
<style>
  /* Unknown and custom elements are INLINE by default,
     so they need a display value to lay out properly. */
  user-card {
    display: block;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

  <user-card>
    <strong>Pankaj</strong><br>
    Java Developer
  </user-card>

  <script>
    // A real custom element with behaviour attached
    class GreetBox extends HTMLElement {
      connectedCallback() {
        const who = this.getAttribute("name") || "friend";
        this.textContent = "Hello, " + who + "!";
        this.style.display = "block";
        this.style.padding = "8px";
        this.style.background = "#eef";
      }
    }
    // The hyphen in the name is REQUIRED
    customElements.define("greet-box", GreetBox);

    const box = document.createElement("greet-box");
    box.setAttribute("name", "Student");
    document.body.appendChild(box);
  </script>

</body>
</html>`,
                                    ]
                                }
                            ],
