                                    content: `<h3>5. Advantages (Advanced)</h3>
• <b>Cacheable and shareable</b> - a stable absolute URL can be cached by a CDN and shared safely.<br>
• <b>Content addressable</b> - fingerprinted asset URLs such as app.a3f9c1.js allow year-long cache headers.<br>
• <b>Required by protocols</b> - RSS, sitemaps, JSON-LD structured data and OAuth redirect URIs all demand absolute URLs.<br><br>

<h3>6. Disadvantages (Advanced)</h3>
• <b>Environment coupling</b> - hard-coded domains are the single biggest cause of staging leaks into production.<br>
• <b>Protocol downgrade</b> - an http absolute URL on an https page is blocked, silently breaking images and scripts.<br>
• <b>Open redirect vulnerability</b> - accepting an absolute URL from a query parameter and redirecting to it lets an attacker send users to a phishing site using <i>your</i> trusted domain in the link.<br>
• <b>SSRF risk</b> on the server - fetching a user-supplied absolute URL can expose internal services.<br>
• <b>Scheme-relative URLs are now discouraged</b>, because everything should simply be https.`,
