                                    content: `<h3>1. Definition</h3>
&bull; <b>sizes</b> tells the browser <b>how wide the image box will be</b> on the page, at different screen widths.<br>
&bull; It is written as a list of <b>media condition and length</b> pairs, with a final length that has no condition.<br>
&bull; Example: sizes equal to (max-width: 600px) 100vw, 400px means full screen width on phones and 400 pixels otherwise.<br>
&bull; It is used <b>together with srcset w descriptors</b>. Without sizes, those w values are almost useless.<br>
<h3>2. Purpose of This Topic</h3>
&bull; To help the browser choose the right file <b>before</b> the CSS is applied.<br>
&bull; To avoid downloading a 1200 pixel file for a 200 pixel thumbnail.<br>
&bull; To make one image tag work correctly in a one column mobile layout and a four column desktop grid.<br>
<h3>3. Need of This Topic</h3>
&bull; The browser starts downloading images very early, before it knows the CSS layout, so we must tell it the width.<br>
&bull; A missing sizes means the browser assumes 100vw and downloads a file that is far too big.<br>
&bull; It is the part of responsive images that beginners get wrong most often.<br>`,
