                                    content: `<h3>4. Advantages</h3>
&bull; <b>Accurate defaults.</b> You get the exact browser value instead of guessing numbers like <b>0.83em</b> for a heading margin.<br>
&bull; <b>Respects the user.</b> If the reader has custom accessibility styles, <b>revert</b> honours them, while hard coded values do not.<br>
&bull; <b>Perfect for prose.</b> One small block of <b>revert</b> rules turns a reset page into readable article content.<br>
&bull; <b>Undo without deleting.</b> You can neutralise a framework rule locally, leaving the framework untouched.<br>
<h3>5. Disadvantages</h3>
&bull; <b>Browsers differ.</b> Chrome, Firefox and Safari have slightly different default stylesheets, so <b>revert</b> can produce small visual differences.<br>
&bull; <b>Not reproducible in tests.</b> Screenshot tests can flake if the headless browser default differs from the developer browser.<br>
&bull; <b>Only reverts author styles.</b> It cannot undo a user stylesheet or a browser default, which sometimes confuses people.<br>
&bull; <b>Older browser support.</b> Very old browsers do not support it, so a fallback declaration may be needed.<br>
&bull; <b>all: revert is broad</b> and can bring back unwanted browser chrome such as native button gradients.<br>`,
